1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,400
This isn't a plane, it's a car.

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:08,470
Tesla's new Roadster launched off the top of a ramp upside down and glided back to the ground on its thrusters.

3
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:16,510
That same night, on the livestream, it went from zero to 60 mph, about 97 km/h, in 0.81 seconds.

4
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:19,100
So how does this car actually fly?

5
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:22,850
In this video we explain it entirely from Tesla's official statements.

6
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:25,450
Welcome to BosNRoll.

7
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:27,770
Part one: What happened?

8
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:35,046
Tesla unveiled the second-generation Roadster on the night of October 15 at SpaceX's rocket test site

9
00:00:35,046 --> 00:00:35,530
in McGregor, Texas.

10
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:41,807
On stage were Elon Musk, chief designer Franz von Holzhausen and Lars Moravy,

11
00:00:41,807 --> 00:00:42,520
vice president of vehicle engineering.

12
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:48,920
Musk presented the car as a joint effort between the world's best car company and the best rocket company.

13
00:00:49,270 --> 00:00:53,390
He also gave a warning: success is not guaranteed, but excitement is 100 percent guaranteed.

14
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:56,350
The Roadster is a two-seat sports car.

15
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:02,100
According to the design team, its inspirations are the SR-71 reconnaissance plane and the F-35 fighter jet.

16
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:06,220
To save weight, the seats are molded directly into the chassis.

17
00:01:06,570 --> 00:01:11,290
Steering, brakes and throttle are fully electronic: a steer-by-wire system.

18
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:13,850
Part two: How does it work?

19
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:19,309
The system that lifts the Roadster has three parts: suction fans under the car,

20
00:01:19,309 --> 00:01:21,530
high-pressure air tanks and thruster nozzles.

21
00:01:22,330 --> 00:01:27,930
The first part is the fan system. Fans under the car rapidly push out the air between the car and the ground.

22
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:32,320
Pressure underneath drops, and the atmospheric pressure above presses the car onto the surface.

23
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:38,190
According to Tesla, this system can generate up to 2 G, twice the car's own weight.

24
00:01:38,990 --> 00:01:42,750
That's why the car could hang completely upside down on the ramp.

25
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,700
In Musk's joking words: this car really sucks.

26
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,540
This isn't just a stunt.

27
00:01:49,890 --> 00:01:55,410
According to Tesla's engineers, tires alone typically provide about 1.26 G of grip.

28
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,640
The fan system pushes that above 2 G in corners.

29
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:06,347
The idea isn't new: in 1978, the Brabham BT46B Formula 1 car also used a fan to stick to the track,

30
00:02:06,347 --> 00:02:08,390
and was withdrawn after a single race.

31
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:12,830
The second part is technology from SpaceX: cold-gas thrusters.

32
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:16,100
The car carries two carbon-overwrapped high-pressure tanks.

33
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:22,890
According to Tesla, they hold 600 liters in total and about 250 kilograms of air.

34
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,090
That air is blasted out through 10 nozzles.

35
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:31,760
There are four main thrusters at the rear; smaller thrusters on the body control the car's direction and rotation.

36
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:34,470
Just like a rocket's attitude control system.

37
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:40,928
The physics is simple. As compressed air passes through a nozzle that narrows and then widens,

38
00:02:40,928 --> 00:02:41,790
it speeds up past the speed of sound.

39
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:46,700
By Newton's third law, as air shoots backward, the car is pushed forward or upward.

40
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:49,650
No fuel, no combustion: just compressed air.

41
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:53,650
In this shot you can see the four rear thrusters firing.

42
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,240
The bright rings in the exhaust are called Mach diamonds; they show the gas is moving faster than sound.

43
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:03,310
According to Tesla, these thrusters produce more than 80 kilonewtons of thrust.

44
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:10,070
For comparison, 80 kilonewtons is enough force to lift roughly 8 tonnes.

45
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:15,769
No special fuel is needed to fill the tanks; according to Tesla, the car can

46
00:03:15,769 --> 00:03:16,580
refill them by itself.

47
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:19,380
Part three: Does it really fly?

48
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:25,860
In the demo, the car launched off a ramp, flipped in mid-air and jumped over a row of parked Teslas.

49
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:28,930
Tesla compared the move to a skateboard kickflip.

50
00:03:29,730 --> 00:03:32,890
So could this car fly for minutes like a helicopter?

51
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:35,720
Short answer: no. Let's do a rough calculation.

52
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:39,630
Compressed-air thrusters are far less efficient than rocket propellants.

53
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:45,420
With 250 kilograms of air, full thrust of 80 kilonewtons can only last a few seconds.

54
00:03:45,770 --> 00:03:48,130
By our estimate, about 2 seconds.

55
00:03:48,930 --> 00:03:51,170
So this is not a plane or a helicopter.

56
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:56,800
It's a rocket-assisted car designed for short hops, mid-air flips and soft landings.

57
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:02,840
Musk said it plainly on stage: this is not a safety-optimized car.

58
00:04:03,190 --> 00:04:06,990
He added that he isn't sure these features will be legal in every jurisdiction.

59
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:10,900
The car in the demo was driven autonomously, so nobody would get hurt.

60
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,540
Part four: Price and timing.

61
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:19,300
On the livestream, the Roadster went from zero to 60 mph in 0.81 seconds.

62
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:26,250
Musk said the top speed will be over 230 mph, about 370 km/h.

63
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:29,410
Tesla offers three options for reservations.

64
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:35,680
A $50,000 deposit for the Performance version and $250,000 for the Thrust version with thrusters.

65
00:04:36,030 --> 00:04:40,550
The single-seat Rocket version with the full flight system will be sold by invitation only.

66
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:42,700
The sale price hasn't been announced yet.

67
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:46,700
Series production is planned to start in 2028.

68
00:04:47,050 --> 00:04:53,530
A reminder: the Roadster was first unveiled in 2017, with deliveries promised for 2020.

69
00:04:54,330 --> 00:05:00,108
According to Musk, the Roadster will be the only manually driven car in Tesla's increasingly driverless

70
00:05:00,108 --> 00:05:00,490
lineup.

71
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:03,720
In Tesla's words: the last best driver's car.

72
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:07,800
Our take: calling it a flying car is a bit of marketing.

73
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:10,870
This car doesn't fly, it jumps for a few seconds.

74
00:05:11,220 --> 00:05:14,540
But the engineering behind it is real and follows the laws of physics.

75
00:05:14,890 --> 00:05:17,610
The real question is whether it will be street legal.

76
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:20,410
Would you ride in this car?

77
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:24,840
Tell us in the comments. Don't forget to subscribe to BosNRoll. See you next time.
